 "cases": [
  {
   "id": "G1",
   "name": "agree-email",
   "purpose": "on email-Eu-core, the parallel SpMV and 10 CG iterations (classic and pipelined; 2-5 ranks, threads; rows/nnz partitions; alltoallv/neighbor/overlap exchange; natural and BFS order) equal the 1-rank run to rounding, and converged parallel answers are within the condition-number bound of the 1-rank answer",
   "why": "SpMV: every y_i = (d_i + a) x_i - sum of the neighbours' x_j is the same floating-point sum in every partition and exchange (rows keep their neighbour order), so natural-order layouts agree bitwise and the BFS-numbered file, whose rows list the same neighbours in another order, agrees to rounding (~1e-15); 1e-14 leaves a margin, while one missing ghost changes y_i by a whole x_j. CG after 10 iterations: runs differ only in the order in which the dot products' partial sums are added (~1e-15 here); 1e-10 is far above that and far below the effect of one wrong ghost. Measured on email-Eu-core, that rounding difference grows about 1000x every 5 iterations once CG has resolved the hub eigenvalues (1e-13 at 15, 1e-10 at 20, 1e-7 at 25 iterations; also between 1 and 2 threads on one rank), so a fixed-iteration comparison is sharp only up to ~10 iterations. Converged answers are compared like V5: ||x_P - x_1||/||x_1|| <= c (rho_P + rho_1)/(1 - c rho_1), rho = true relative residual, with c = (2 d_max + a)/a >= cond(L + aI) (Gershgorin: lambda_max(L) <= 2 d_max)"
  },
  {
   "id": "G2",
   "name": "dense-tiny",
   "purpose": "on a hand-made graph (two 5-cliques, a bridge, a tail, a self-loop, a duplicate edge), the converter's counts are as constructed and x matches a dense solve on 1-3 ranks; labels are all correct",
   "why": "for the tiny graph cond(L + aI) <= (2 d_max + a)/a = 21 (d_max = 5, a = 0.5), and the solves stop at a relative residual of 1e-13, so the relative error is at most ~21 x 1e-13 = 2e-12; 1e-10 leaves a 50x margin; a lost ghost or a wrong edge changes x by more than 1e-3"
  }
 ],
